{"y":1994,"label":"1993–94","coach":"Jim Calhoun","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":29,"l":5,"cw":16,"cl":2,"confFinish":"1st in Big East","finish":"sweet16","seed":2,"region":"East","apHigh":2,"apFinal":4,"srs":22.58,"sos":6.26,"ortg":109.1,"drtg":88.1,"ppg":84.9,"oppg":68.6,"story":{"headline":"Donyell Marshall's Big East","text":"With junior Donyell Marshall at the peak of his powers and freshmen Ray Allen and Doron Sheffer arriving, UConn went 29–5 and 16–2 to win the Big East regular-season title. Marshall was the unanimous Big East Player of the Year, the league's Defensive Player of the Year and a consensus first-team All-American; Sheffer was Big East Rookie of the Year and Allen made the All-Freshman team, while Calhoun won Big East Coach of the Year. The Huskies rose as high as No. 2, highlighted by a 77–36 demolition at No. 12 Virginia. Providence edged UConn 69–67 in the Big East semifinals, but the Huskies beat Rider and George Washington to reach the Sweet Sixteen, where Florida won 69–60 in overtime. Marshall left for the NBA as the No. 4 overall pick.","moments":[{"date":"1994-03-17","title":"NCAA First Round: W vs Rider","text":"UConn beat Rider 64–46 in the NCAA tournament first round at Nassau Veterans Memorial Coliseum, Uniondale, New York."},{"date":"1994-03-19","title":"NCAA Second Round: W vs George Washington","text":"UConn beat George Washington 75–63 in the NCAA tournament second round at Nassau Veterans Memorial Coliseum, Uniondale, New York."},{"date":"1994-03-25","title":"NCAA Sweet Sixteen: L vs Florida","text":"UConn lost to Florida 60–69 (OT) in the NCAA tournament sweet sixteen at Miami Arena, Miami, Florida."}],"honors":["Big East regular-season Champions","Donyell Marshall – Consensus first-team All-American","Donyell Marshall – Big East Player of the Year","Jim Calhoun – Big East Coach of the Year","Donyell Marshall – Big East Defensive Player of the Year","Doron Sheffer – Big East Rookie/Freshman of the Year","Donyell 